Resume mistakes that instantly disqualify you ( I’ve seen it happen )
I work as a resume writer so I see a lot of resumes that get rejected and a lot of the reasons are things people have no idea are even problems. This isn’t generic advice, it’s what actually gets people filtered out before their qualifications get read.

Using your work email to apply

Applying from

jsmith@currentemployer.com is one of the worst things you can do. Shows terrible judgment and makes recruiters question whether you’d be careless with their company too. Always personal email. A hiring manager once showed me a resume they rejected purely because the candidate applied from their current company email and they said “that’s all I needed to see.”

Current job written in past tense

Bullets for your current role say “managed” and “led” instead of “manage” and “lead.” Recruiters think you already left or you’re about to. Current job is present tense, everything else is past. Simple but messing it up creates immediate confusion about whether you’re even still employed.

PDF won’t open or is password protected

File’s corrupted or password protected without the password included. Recruiter can’t open it, moves on. They’re not troubleshooting your file. Test it before sending. Client lost an interview slot because their PDF needed Adobe Pro to open and the company used basic readers.

Filename is

“Resume_FINAL_v3_UPDATED.pdf”

Looks disorganized before they even open it. FirstName_LastName_Resume.pdf is standard. A hiring manager mentioned to me once that seeing messy filenames made them assume the candidate’s work would be equally disorganized.

Job title on resume doesn’t match LinkedIn

Resume says “Senior Marketing Manager” but LinkedIn says “Marketing Coordinator.” Recruiters check both and assume you’re lying somewhere. Keep them identical. Caught someone mid-interview process when the recruiter pulled up their LinkedIn and the titles didn’t match. They didn’t move forward.

Unprofessional voicemail greeting

Your voicemail is a joke or has music blasting. Recruiter calls, hears it, doesn’t leave a message. Change it to just your name or something professional. Missed interview invite because of this and the candidate had no idea why they never heard back.

Sending a Google Docs share link instead of a file

You send the share link instead of downloading as PDF. Permissions break, recruiter sees “Request Access,” moves to the next person. Always send actual files, not links.

Employment gap that’s obvious but you tried to hide it with year-only dates

Job ended December 2022, next started January 2024. You listed just years hoping no one would notice the 13-month gap. They notice. If there’s a gap just add a one-line explanation or show what you did during it. Trying to hide obvious gaps looks worse than just addressing them.

Listing skills you’ve never actually used professionally

“Expert in Python” but zero jobs involved coding. Recruiters see it doesn’t match your history and assume you’re padding or lying. Only list what you’ve genuinely used and can talk about in detail if asked.

Putting salary requirements on your resume

“Salary requirement: $90K” or “Expected range: $80-100K.” You either priced yourself out or you’re showing you don’t understand norms. Salary gets discussed in interviews, not on your resume. Ever.

Using extremely casual language

“Crushed sales targets” or “Totally rebuilt the site.” Resume isn’t a text to your friend. Keep it professional. “Exceeded sales targets 35%” and “Redesigned website, improved load time 60%” work without sounding like informal chat.

“Proficient in Google” listed as a skill

Not Google Analytics or Google Ads. Just “Google.” That’s not a skill in 2026, it’s baseline. Shows you’re padding because you don’t have actual technical tools worth listing.

Jobs split across page breaks

Title and company at bottom of page one, all your work described on page two. Recruiter skims page one, doesn’t flip, thinks that’s everything. Keep each job on one page together. Format intentionally.

This is for corporate hiring mostly. Marketing, tech, finance, ops, consulting where resumes go through multiple people and details get scrutinized. Other fields have different standards.

I fix these mistakes regularly and rejection patterns change when they’re cleaned up. Doesn’t guarantee anything because hiring’s a mess right now but at least you’re not getting cut for things you didn’t even realize mattered.

Good luck.​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​

