Rookie mistakes I have seen so far:

listing experiences from older to newer rather than newer to older

listing that one summer job in the library 5 years ago (it was for a science position, so not relevant)

copy pasted the same text of activities between PhD and Post Doc and the worst part was that they wrote generic stuff like "supported ongoing projects". I had no clue which field, what methods and technologied they used and what impact they had. Copy pasting within your resume is bad because space is soooo precious, dont waste it like that

resume written in ONE BLOCK OF TEXT instead of bullet points

2 pages of publications and conferences they've been to

a table within a table, this is not ATS friendly

typos and it was clearly written in a different language first and then translated because they forgot to translate some sections

comic sans 

AI Resume Writing Tips
Quick Rules To Use AI Effectively
Use AI As A First Draft Tool. "AI picks which of my bullets go in, I write the words."

Never Send AI Output Word-For-Word. "If you're using AI output without supervision and editing, you are using AI wrong."

Keep A Master Bullet Bank You Control. "Keeping one master resume with every bullet I've ever written... AI only matches it against the posting."

Tailoring And ATS Practical Steps
Make Tailoring Cheap Not Lazy. "tailored beats volume. Almost all my interviews came from the tailored pile. But tailoring took me 5 minutes with that setup, not 15, so I did it for most"

Use Simple, ATS-Safe Formatting. "Single column, standard headings, no tables/text boxes/icons."

Match JD Language Honestly, Then Add Specifics. "Upload JD, pick relevant bullets and rewrite in JD's language; never invent metrics, ask me for numbers."

Tone, Humanness, And Recruiter Signals
Put Concrete Nouns And Numbers Back In. "Put the real nouns back in... Tools, systems, customers, ticket types, reports, products."

Avoid Generic Buzzword Phrases. "Recruiters spot resumes that read like they have no specific memory of doing the job."

Edit For Voice — Read Aloud Test. "Read it out loud once before sending."

Tools And Workflows People Recommend
Claude Or ChatGPT For Drafting, Then Humanize. "Claude tends to be stronger for rewriting... ChatGPT is more reliable for iterative tailoring."

Use Platforms For Tracking/Tailoring If Volume Matters. "its AI helps you dynamically toggle bullet points on and off to spawn highly"

Check ATS Match But Don’t Rely Only On Score. "Run it through Jobscan to compare wording against the JD and fix issues."

